141
00:12:21,410 --> 00:12:28,468
Historical Archives, Beijing, reprint in Zhongguo Gudai Ditu Ji中国古代地图集 (Beijing, 1990).

142
00:12:28,468 --> 00:12:31,963
Facsimile from the Shanghai Museum of Navigation 航海博物馆.

143
00:12:31,963 --> 00:12:36,491
Photograph by the author. This section of the map of the Great Ming depicts the

144
00:12:36,491 --> 00:12:42,668
western portion of Korea and displays the Manchu place-name transliterations on affixed

145
00:12:42,668 --> 00:12:47,788
labels. In some places, over time, the labels have detached, revealing the original

146
00:12:47,788 --> 00:12:50,458
Chinese text underneath.

147
00:12:50,458 --> 00:12:56,517
Repairs, modifications, as well as annotations therefore, all belonged to a broad

148
00:12:56,517 --> 00:13:02,195
strategy of translation of cartographic artifacts first practiced in the preconquest

149
00:13:02,195 --> 00:13:07,129
period on the map by Li Yingshi and Ricci, then on the Map of the

150
00:13:07,129 --> 00:13:12,051
nine borders, and ultimately, after the conquest, on Ming maps.

151
00:13:12,051 --> 00:13:17,625
This demonstrates the continuity in translation across the Ming-Qing divide.

152
00:13:17,625 --> 00:13:22,988
The presence of a map co-authored by Matteo Ricci in the Shenyang palace is ultimately

153
00:13:22,988 --> 00:13:28,375
a testament to the extent of knowledge networks in the early modern period.
